From f86eed8f3c0d49f3a27e81bb847150865e3c1f4b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 15:49:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Normalize GEO coexpression scores with Mutual Rank instead of
 a one-sided percentile

A live run against MA_4115 (351 candidates, after the query old_locus_tag
fallback fix made STRING/GEO evidence actually reach this query) found
coexpression_gse77738/coexpression_gse64349 scoring roughly half of all
candidates above the 0.7 percentile -- a properly discriminating signal
should center near 0.5. The two datasets' percentiles also correlated at
r=0.549 across 291 shared candidates despite being independent
experiments, too much agreement to be query-specific signal. STRING's own
string_cooccurrence/string_neighborhood, by contrast, stayed properly
sparse (median 0) over the same candidate set.

Root cause: normalized_value was each pair's correlation percentile-ranked
only against the QUERY gene's own background distribution. That answers
"does the query find this candidate unusually correlated?" but never asks
the symmetric question "does the candidate find the query unusually
correlated?" -- so a candidate that is simply broadly correlated with much
of the genome (a coexpression "hub", commonly just a highly and precisely
measured gene, for reasons that have nothing to do with the query) still
scores high from the query's side alone.

Fix: normalized_value is now each pair's Mutual Rank -- the geometric mean
of the correlation's percentile from the query's side and from the
candidate's own side (the same idea CoNekT/ATTED-II-style coexpression
network tools use, adapted to already-percentile-ranked values here). A
hub's own background is elevated too, so its candidate-side percentile
deflates even when its query-side percentile looks fine; a truly specific
partner keeps a high percentile on both sides. This is a normalization
requested explicitly in place of exclusion: GSE77738 stays in the score
(Phase 6b's separate, still-valid decision to exclude it specifically from
interaction_score's sum is unaffected).

Implementation:
- analysis/coexpression_bridge.py: split the old CoexpressionPairValue into
  an internal _OneSidedCoexpression (unchanged on-disk cache shape) and a
  new public CoexpressionPairValue carrying query_percentile/
  candidate_percentile/percentile (their geometric mean). CoexpressionBundle
  now retains the z-score matrix so lookup() can derive a candidate's own
  background on demand, memoized in-process only (not persisted to the
  JsonCache -- candidates number in the hundreds per run, unlike the
  handful of configured queries the disk cache is sized for; the
  matrix-vector product itself is cheap enough not to need persisting).
  Existing on-disk cache files from before this change keep working
  unchanged.
- analysis/interaction_scoring.py: explanation text now reports mutual/
  query-side/candidate-side percentiles together for auditability.
- tests/test_coexpression_bridge.py: new
  test_gse77738_mutual_rank_deflates_hub_genes constructs a synthetic
  hub-vs-specific-partner dataset and checks the hub's mutual rank drops
  below its own one-sided percentile and below the true partner's.
- claude/coexpression_mutual_rank_normalization.md records the real-data
  finding, root cause, and design decision.

Also noted (not fixed here, out of scope): both coexpression loaders
unconditionally re-read/re-download their source file before ever checking
whether a query's result is already cached, defeating the cache when the
source file is absent -- a pre-existing, independent bug already caught by
tests/test_coexpression_bridge.py::test_result_is_cached_and_reused_without_reparsing
and tests/test_interaction_scoring.py::test_coexpression_gse77738_reuses_cache_across_runs
(both fail identically on unmodified main, confirmed before this commit).

Full suite: 503 passed, the same 2 pre-existing failures above (unrelated
to this change) still failing.

